{
 "id": "monty-hall",
 "channel": "philosophy",
 "style": "clay",
 "title": "THE MONTY HALL PROBLEM",
 "angle": "Why does a simple game-show puzzle fool even brilliant people?",
 "answer": "Your first pick is right only one time in three, and the host, who knows where the car is, never opens it, so switching wins two times in three; people resist because the two closed doors feel like a fifty-fifty, while trained pigeons simply learn from outcomes; and the real Monty Hall warned that real hosts don't follow fixed rules.",
 "logline": "Pigeons beat people at this puzzle, so why does it fool us? Three doors, one car, and the host shows a goat, so switching wins two times in three, but in 1990 about 10,000 readers told the columnist who said so that she was wrong, and a great mathematician held out until a simulation, because your first pick is right only a third of the time, so the host is pointing at the car, but the real Monty Hall said hosts don't play by fixed rules, so ask why you're being offered the switch.",
 "hook_text": "PIGEONS BEAT PEOPLE",
 "hooks": [
  "About 10,000 readers wrote in to say this answer was wrong. It wasn't.",
  "Switch doors, and you win two times in three."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "room"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "Pigeons learn to solve this puzzle better than most people.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "After a month of practice, they switched 96 percent of the time; humans, about two-thirds.",
   "say": "After a month of practice, they switched ninety-six percent of the time; humans, about two-thirds.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So why does a simple game-show puzzle fool so many smart people?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Three doors: a car behind one, goats behind the other two.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "You pick a door; the host, who knows, opens another and shows a goat.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Should you switch? Yes: switching wins two times in three.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But in 1990, when a magazine columnist said so, about 10,000 readers wrote to tell her she was wrong.",
   "say": "But in nineteen ninety, when a magazine columnist said so, about ten thousand readers wrote to tell her she was wrong.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Nearly a thousand of them had PhDs.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "One of the century's great mathematicians refused to believe it until he saw a computer simulation.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "The trick: your first pick is right only one time in three.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "So two times in three, the car is behind the other doors, and the host never opens it.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Picture 100 doors: you pick one, he opens 98 goats. Would you stay?",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the real Monty Hall said the puzzle missed something.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "On his show, he didn't always offer a switch, and used offers to play with contestants' nerves.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the maths is simple only when the rules are fixed.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "When someone offers you a second choice, do you ask why they're offering it?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
